{"name":"Moonlit Sonata","hex":"#37525f","fetched":"2026-10-18","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"moonlit","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Adjective","definitions":["Lit by moonlight."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/moonlit"},{"term":"sonata","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A musical composition for one or a few instruments, one of which is frequently a piano, in three or four movements that vary in key and tempo."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/sonata"}],"entries":[{"title":"Moonlight","blocks":[{"text":"Moonlight is light from the surface of the Moon, consisting mostly of reflected sunlight, and some earthlight."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/d/dc/Earthshine_Moon.jpg/330px-Earthshine_Moon.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Moonlight","wikidata":"Q1058800"},{"title":"Sonata","blocks":[{"text":"In music, a sonata is a piece that consists of 3 or 4 movements that can be for different musical instruments. The term evolved through the history of music, designating a variety of forms until the Classical era, when it took on increasing importance. Sonata is a vague term, with varying meanings depending on the context and time period. By the early 19th century it came to represent a principle of composing large-scale works. It was applied to most instrumental genres and regarded—alongside the fugue—as one of two fundamental methods of organizing, interpreting and analyzing concert music. Though the musical style of sonatas has changed since the Classical era, most 20th- and 21st-century sonatas maintain the overarching structure."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/2a/Beethoven_opus_101_manuscript.jpg/330px-Beethoven_opus_101_manuscript.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Sonata","wikidata":"Q131269"}],"images":[{"srcset